    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d589">

                <pb facs="0646" n="548" xml:id="pg548"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">493.11 Ekvall, Henry/53: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Assistant Secretary of State (Carr) to the Minister in China (Johnson)</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1932-11-05T15:00:00-05:00">November 5, 1932—3
                        p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>362. Your 1234, November 5, 2 p.m. Department feels that you should send to
                        Stanton at once a
                    paraphrase of Department’s 361, November 4, 5 p.m. with directions to present
                    full demands to the local Chinese authorities and at the same time to inform the
                    representatives of the Nanking Government at Sian of demands; and that you
                    should instruct Peck to file for record with Nanking
                    authorities the full demands. Department feels also that the question of
                    accepting any offer made by Chinese authorities to pay immediate compensation
                    for American property losses suffered remains open pending Stanton’s presentation of full
                    demands and his report with regard to the attitude of the Chinese
                    authorities.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                    >Carr</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
